<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      Only the keys we need to ADD or OVERRIDE live here; xtool generates the rest
      and merges this over the top. Referenced from xtool.yml via `infoPath`.

      NSAllowsLocalNetworking exists because the dev API is served over plain HTTP
      on the LAN (http://192.168.100.136/api/v1/). App Transport Security blocks
      cleartext HTTP by default, and the symptom is a transport failure that looks
      like broken Swift rather than a policy decision.

      This is deliberately the NARROW exception rather than NSAllowsArbitraryLoads:
      it permits local-network resources (private IPv4 ranges, .local, and
      unqualified hostnames) and nothing else. A deployed environment should use
      HTTPS and drop this key entirely.
    -->
    <key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
    <dict>
        <key>NSAllowsLocalNetworking</key>
        <true/>
    </dict>

    <!--
      WHY THIS IS REQUIRED, and it is the reason sign-in could not reach the API.

      Since iOS 14, an app needs explicit local-network permission to open a connection
      to a private IP address (10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16). Without this key present
      iOS denies the connection, and it fails looking exactly like a dead server: the
      API is reachable from the Mac or PC on the same address, the firewall is open, and
      the device still gets nothing.

      NSAllowsLocalNetworking above permits cleartext HTTP to those ranges, but it does
      NOT grant the local-network permission -- the two are separate switches and both
      are needed.

      iOS shows a permission prompt the first time the app touches the LAN; if the user
      declines, reset it under Settings > Privacy & Security > Local Network.
    -->
    <key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Chmaba POS connects to your store's local server to load products and record sales.</string>

    <key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
    <string>Chmaba POS</string>

    <!-- Portrait only. A till is held or mounted upright; landscape buys nothing
         and costs us a layout pass. Matches the Android client's own restriction. -->
    <key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
    <array>
        <string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
